@simonklee It seems like the fix for this issue has completely removed the "Modified Files" section (see issue [#30877](https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/issues/30877)).
Could this be the case or is this unrelated?

<img width="1874" height="968" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f53a03b0-cf78-45ad-b2c7-adf5d79fad3d" />

// Line 573-575: Get extension → look up LANGUAGE_EXTENSIONS table → get languageId
const extension = path.extname(request.path)
const languageId = LANGUAGE_EXTENSIONS[extension] ?? "plaintext"
//                              ↑ Custom extensions (.R, .gd etc.) not in table → falls back to "plaintext"
// Line 636: Sent to LSP server, languageId is already "plaintext"
await connection.sendNotification("textDocument/didOpen", {
  textDocument: {
    uri: pathToFileURL(request.path).href,
    languageId,  // ← Sends "plaintext", R languageserver ignores it
    version: 0,
    text,
  },
})
The LANGUAGE_EXTENSIONS map in language.ts:
".r": "r",     // lowercase .r exists, languageId = "r" ✅
".R": ???      // uppercase .R missing, languageId = "plaintext" ❌
This is the root cause. The fix is to allow languageId to be specified in the LSP config instead of hardcoding the lookup table.

I’m seeing the same issue on v1.17.4. I used an AI to help trace the execution flow, and it pointed out a likely race condition in the initialization sequence. Here is the breakdown:

The `--pure` flag fails because of a timing race between Yargs middleware and Effect's `ConfigProvider`.

### Root Cause
In `packages/opencode/src/index.ts`, `OPENCODE_PURE` is set inside the Yargs middleware. However, Effect’s `ConfigProvider` snapshots `process.env` on the first read (during module loading). By the time the middleware runs, the snapshot is already taken and `flags.pure` defaults to `false`.

There’s actually a comment in `packages/opencode/src/server/server.ts` acknowledging this exact caching behavior:
```typescript
// Effect's default ConfigProvider snapshots process.env on first read and caches the
// result on a module-singleton Reference...
```

### Suggested Fix
Move the environment variable assignment to the top of `packages/opencode/src/index.ts`, before the Yargs instance is created:

```typescript
const args = hideBin(process.argv)

// Set this BEFORE yargs and other modules load to beat the Effect snapshot
if (args.includes("--pure")) {
  process.env.OPENCODE_PURE = "1"
}

const cli = yargs(args)
// ...
```

### Temporary Workaround
Until this is patched, you can bypass the flag and set the env var directly:
```bash
OPENCODE_PURE=1 opencode
```
